1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,380
A farming region has grown crops for decades. It has output and development projects,

2
00:00:05,740 --> 00:00:10,220
but firms still make no profit. Repeated efforts to set quality standards and develop value-added processing

3
00:00:10,540 --> 00:00:14,520
keep hitting obstacles. Why has the industry stalled here?

4
00:00:15,320 --> 00:00:17,220
Let's use a fictional teaching case

5
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:22,040
to see how professional brand strategy analysis untangles the problem.

6
00:00:22,660 --> 00:00:26,840
When we arrive, officials say there are no lead firms; firms say there is no market.

7
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:31,320
Farmers say investing isn't worthwhile. Each group has its own experience

8
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:35,400
and perspective. Everyone sees just one piece of the puzzle.

9
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:39,760
How do we fit the pieces together? Follow each claim to the evidence

10
00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:41,660
and connect the evidence into a chain.

11
00:00:42,360 --> 00:00:45,520
We start by tracing more than a decade of turning points in the industry.

12
00:00:46,240 --> 00:00:49,540
We ask entrepreneurs about a processing project that never got off the ground:

13
00:00:49,980 --> 00:00:54,060
What information blocked progress? Who withdrew their investment, and when?

14
00:00:54,620 --> 00:00:57,960
Then we speak to the officials involved and to farmers, checking verbal agreements

15
00:00:58,280 --> 00:01:02,200
against purchase records and the calculations made at the time.

16
00:01:02,860 --> 00:01:05,020
Next, we look beyond the region to its peers.

17
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:08,660
Why do other regions with similar conditions make it work,

18
00:01:09,080 --> 00:01:12,520
while this one keeps hitting the same obstacle? We compare

19
00:01:12,840 --> 00:01:15,600
market, technical and investment conditions.

20
00:01:16,020 --> 00:01:20,040
We also examine failures and exits, then link records across several years.

21
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:21,900
Only then does the problem begin to emerge.

22
00:01:22,340 --> 00:01:26,320
In this case, other regions have a mechanism to share price risk.

23
00:01:26,860 --> 00:01:29,440
Local processors need reliable deliveries.

24
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:33,840
Farmers want market rates when prices rise, and firms to guarantee a minimum price when they fall.

25
00:01:34,460 --> 00:01:37,580
Guaranteed minimum prices also leave firms bearing the risk of falling prices.

26
00:01:38,340 --> 00:01:41,180
Both sides avoid risks they cannot afford to bear,

27
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:45,620
so long-term contracts are hard to honor. Each side has reasons for its choices,

28
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:47,600
yet cooperation remains hard to sustain.

29
00:01:48,020 --> 00:01:50,700
Only now is there evidence for a diagnosis:

30
00:01:51,140 --> 00:01:53,220
these processing projects keep stalling

31
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:58,000
because the need for reliable supply and the way risk is shared do not align.

32
00:01:58,680 --> 00:02:01,640
First, test the rules of cooperation. Then compare what it would take and cost

33
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:06,680
to add sales channels or invest in processing lines. Set out the choices in a three-year action plan.

34
00:02:07,340 --> 00:02:11,400
You may now wonder: how can a newcomer conduct such rigorous analysis based on fieldwork

35
00:02:11,720 --> 00:02:14,480
without years of experience?

36
00:02:14,960 --> 00:02:18,960
That is why we developed the Brand Strategy Analysis Skill.

37
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:22,720
I have personally spent more than 150 days on field research over the years.

38
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:26,720
With my team, I researched over ten well-known regional public brands shared by local producers,

39
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:30,860
including Wuchang rice, Yangcheng Lake hairy crab and Huizhou fermented mandarin fish.

40
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:34,460
We distilled fieldwork, expert discussions and analytical judgment

41
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:37,960
into this open-source AI research skill.

42
00:02:38,520 --> 00:02:42,480
It has three layers: task routing, workflows and actionable units.

43
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:47,220
It identifies your current task and directs you to the right workflow,

44
00:02:47,580 --> 00:02:51,040
then breaks each stage into actionable units: concrete steps you can follow.

45
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:55,840
This turns complex expert thinking into methods you can put into practice

46
00:02:56,140 --> 00:02:57,820
and update in light of the evidence.

47
00:02:58,460 --> 00:03:02,360
For newcomers, it helps build detailed interview guides:

48
00:03:02,780 --> 00:03:06,400
whom to talk to, what to ask about, how to follow up on an answer,

49
00:03:06,780 --> 00:03:10,440
and which records to collect. Fieldwork materials go into daily reports.

50
00:03:10,820 --> 00:03:15,740
AI helps find duplicates, check claims, seek counterexamples and locate gaps in the evidence,

51
00:03:16,120 --> 00:03:17,940
then adapt the questions for the next round.

52
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:23,600
For experts, working with AI frees up more attention for key judgments.

53
00:03:24,320 --> 00:03:27,980
AI helps organize basic analyses and cross-check source materials.

54
00:03:28,380 --> 00:03:32,220
Drawing on field experience, you work with it to keep refining the questions and proposals,

55
00:03:32,540 --> 00:03:34,940
developing more creative strategic options.

56
00:03:35,580 --> 00:03:39,620
From an impasse of conflicting accounts to a strategy report with deep insights.

57
00:03:40,020 --> 00:03:44,240
It helps governments and firms clarify why action is needed, who will take it,

58
00:03:44,560 --> 00:03:47,820
and when to reassess, then turn those judgments into an action plan.

59
00:03:48,380 --> 00:03:52,780
The Brand Strategy Analysis Skill translates expert judgment grounded in fieldwork

60
00:03:53,140 --> 00:03:55,480
into practical next steps anyone can follow.
